# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
BLOCKS = {
  "base": [
    ("Why sealing can't fix a dip that starts in the base",
     "<p>A sunken paver almost always points to a problem in the compacted aggregate base underneath it, a soft spot or a void, not to anything a sealer coat can address from the surface. Sealing over a low section locks that dip under a glossy finish rather than correcting it, so releveling, lifting the affected pavers, fixing the base and bedding sand, resetting them level, has to happen before the cleaning and sealing part of the job makes sense. On a {city} property, a contractor who seals first and asks about low spots later has the order of the job backward.</p>"),
    ("How joint sand loss and base erosion feed each other",
     "<p>Joint sand isn't structural, but it does one real job: keeping water from reaching the base directly through the gaps between pavers. Once sand washes thin in a joint, more water gets through to the aggregate below with each storm, and that accelerated erosion is part of why the same joints keep losing sand faster once they start. Re-sanding protects the base indirectly by closing that path, which is why it's paired with cleaning and sealing rather than treated as a separate service on a {city} paver surface.</p>"),
    ("What a rising water table does to joints from underneath",
     "<p>Flatwoods soils common across both the Orlando and Suncoast service areas hold a seasonal high water table within 18 inches of the surface for part of most years. When that water table rises into the compacted base beneath a paver surface, fines and sand can migrate upward through the joints from below, not just wash out from above during a storm. That's one reason a {city} patio or driveway on this kind of ground often needs its joints checked more than once a season, even when nothing about the surface traffic has changed.</p>"),
    ("Why a sealing visit starts with a walk of the whole surface",
     "<p>A sealing job that's done right starts with checking the base condition across the entire paver field, not just inspecting the color and grime on top. Rocking pavers, a soft feel underfoot, or a joint that's visibly lower than its neighbors all point to a base issue that needs addressing before any sealer goes down. Sealing over that kind of problem in {city} just locks a bad surface under a coat that makes the flaw harder to see and, later, harder to fix without redoing the sealer too.</p>"),
    ("Ridge sand holds its joints longer than flatwoods ground does",
     "<p>On the excessively drained ridge sands found west of Orlando, the base rarely saturates enough to push fines or sand up and out through the joints, so a paver surface built on that kind of ground tends to go longer between re-sanding visits. On flatwoods soils with a shallow water table, closer to the surface and common on the Suncoast side too, the same joints lose sand faster and need checking more often. Knowing which kind of ground sits under a given {city} property is a real factor in how often a sealing and re-sanding cycle actually needs repeating.</p>"),
  ],
  "build": [
    ("What cleaning actually accomplishes before anything gets sealed",
     "<p>Pressure washing alone runs roughly $0.35 to $0.80 per square foot and does more than make pavers look better: it strips algae, old sand haze and loose grime that would otherwise trap moisture or dirt under a new sealer coat. A contractor washing a {city} surface is also using that step to judge what's underneath, deciding whether the joints need a full re-sand or just a light top-off, and whether the paver surface itself is in good enough shape to accept a sealer at all. Skipping straight to sealing without this step usually means sealing in whatever was already there.</p>"),
    ("Dry sand or polymeric sand: two different joint materials",
     "<p>Plain dry sand gets swept into the joints and compacted, but it stays loose and can migrate out again over time, especially where water moves through regularly. Polymeric sand works differently: it's swept in dry the same way, then lightly misted with water, which activates a binder that locks it in place and resists washout far better than plain sand does. Neither is required by the paver industry's own guidance, so the choice on a {city} job comes down to how much resistance to washout is worth paying for upfront.</p>"),
    ("Why there's no fixed shelf life printed on a sealer can",
     "<p>Paver sealers don't come with a guaranteed number of years before they wear out; industry guidance says only that a sealer needs reapplication after a period of wear and weather, with acrylic coatings typically holding up for a few years before a recoat is worth doing. That leaves the actual interval to climate, traffic and the specific sealer used rather than to a fixed Florida standard. A {city} driveway that sees daily car traffic and full sun wears through a sealer coat faster than a shaded, lightly used walkway on the same property ever would.</p>"),
    ("The eighth-inch joint a sealer has to respect, not cover over",
     "<p>Paver joints are specified at no more than about 1/8 inch wide, tight enough that the field locks together once sand is compacted into them. A sealer that mostly bridges across the top of that joint, rather than soaking down into the sand itself, changes how the joint behaves under traffic afterward, since a hardened film on top can crack and let water underneath it instead of shedding water the way a properly penetrated joint does. On a {city} job, which sealer type actually gets into the joint, not just onto the paver face, affects how the surface performs for years after.</p>"),
    ("Releveling as a small repeat of the original installation",
     "<p>Fixing a sunken paver means pulling the unit, restoring the bedding sand layer underneath it to its original uncompacted depth, resetting the paver flush with its neighbors, then sweeping fresh sand into the joint around it. That's essentially the original installation spec applied to one paver instead of an entire field, not a patch or a shortcut version of it. Paver repair pricing reflects that real labor, running roughly $7 to $30 per square foot depending on scope, well above what a simple surface clean costs on a {city} property.</p>"),
  ],
  "process": [
    ("Relevel first, then wash, sand and seal, not the other way around",
     "<p>A sealing crew that finds sunken or rocking pavers during the initial walk-through handles releveling before the pressure washer ever comes out, because sealing over a low spot locks the dip in place under a coat that's now harder to touch up without it showing. The order matters more than it sounds: base and bedding repairs happen first, then cleaning, then sand, then sealer, each step depending on the one before it being done right. On a {city} job, skipping straight to sealing because the surface looks mostly fine from a distance is how a small base problem gets sealed in rather than fixed.</p>"),
    ("Washing at an angle instead of straight down into the joints",
     "<p>A pressure washer aimed straight down into a joint strips out whatever sand is left there, which defeats the point of a cleaning pass meant to prep the surface for fresh sand, not empty it further. Crews working a {city} job wash at an angle across the paver field, keeping the wand moving rather than dwelling on one spot, and save the direct, close-range pressure for stains that genuinely need it. That distinction between cleaning the surface and accidentally re-opening every joint is one of the easiest steps to get wrong on a rushed job.</p>"),
    ("Why re-sanding gets compacted before the sealer ever touches it",
     "<p>Dry or polymeric sand swept across a cleaned paver field gets worked into the joints and then compacted, with a plate compactor on a driveway or hand tools on a smaller patio, before any sealer goes down. Sealing over sand that's still loose and only partially settled into the joint traps it in a half-filled gap, which weakens the interlock the sand is supposed to provide once traffic resumes. On a {city} job, that compaction pass is a distinct step in the sequence, not something the sealer coat can substitute for or finish on its own.</p>"),
    ("Giving the surface time to dry before the sealer goes on",
     "<p>A sealer applied to a paver field that's still damp from washing or re-sanding can haze over or trap moisture underneath the coat instead of bonding cleanly. After washing and re-sanding, a {city} job typically pauses long enough for the surface to dry through, which on a property near Orlando can mean working around the median wet-season start of May 27 and the drier stretch that typically follows mid-October, rather than racing the forecast on a day that doesn't cooperate.</p>"),
    ("Sealing in the cooler part of the day, not straight through the heat",
     "<p>A sealer applied under direct midday sun on a hot Florida afternoon can flash off and start setting before it's had a chance to lay down evenly across the paver surface, leaving lap marks or an uneven sheen. Crews working a {city} job often seal earlier or later in the day, when the surface temperature is lower, rather than pushing straight through the schedule regardless of conditions. That timing decision is as much a part of the process as the sealer product itself, and it's one a rushed job skips first.</p>"),
  ],
  "risks": [
    ("Sealing over efflorescence traps it instead of letting it clear",
     "<p>The whitish calcium carbonate bloom that can appear on new concrete pavers within about 60 days of installation doesn't affect the paver's strength, and it typically fades on its own with normal weathering. Sealing over an active bloom before it's had a chance to wear off can trap it under the coat instead, making it harder to clear later than if the surface had simply been left to weather first. On a {city} job with recently installed pavers, waiting a season before the first sealing visit is often the better call than sealing on a fixed schedule regardless of the paver's age.</p>"),
    ("Sealing joint sand before it's had time to compact",
     "<p>Sand swept into the joints right before a sealer coat goes on, without a proper compaction pass first, ends up trapped loose under a hardened surface rather than locked in place the way it should be. That weakens the very interlock the joint sand is meant to provide, and it can show up months later as pavers that shift slightly under traffic even though the surface looks sealed and finished. A {city} job that rushes straight from re-sanding to sealing without pausing to compact is cutting a step that doesn't show its cost until later.</p>"),
    ("A sealer that changes the paver's look more than expected",
     "<p>Not every sealer behaves the same on the surface: a glossy wet-look coating darkens and shines a paver noticeably more than a natural matte finish does, and there's no single Florida standard dictating which look a given product produces. A homeowner who expects a light cleaning and ends up with a visibly darker, glossier {city} driveway was likely sold a sealer type without being shown a sample first. Testing a sealer on a small, less visible section before committing the whole surface to it avoids that kind of surprise.</p>"),
    ("Sealing over a sunken paver highlights the problem instead of hiding it",
     "<p>A glossy sealer coat catches light differently than a dull, unsealed surface does, which means a low or rocking paver that might have gone unnoticed under a matte finish becomes more visible, not less, once the surrounding field has a sheen and the dip doesn't catch the light the same way. Skipping releveling and sealing anyway on a {city} property doesn't just leave the base problem unresolved; it can make the flaw stand out more to anyone looking at the finished surface.</p>"),
    ("A pressure washer held too close can cut into the paver, not just clean it",
     "<p>A wand held too close or lingered on one spot for too long can etch or scar a paver's surface finish, especially on a softer concrete paver compared with a denser natural stone unit, which is a different and more permanent problem than the sand loss a normal cleaning pass causes. That kind of damage shows up as a visibly rough or lighter patch that a sealer coat won't hide. On a {city} job, matching pressure and distance to the specific paver material is part of doing the cleaning step correctly, not an afterthought.</p>"),
  ],
  "care": [
    ("Watching for the sign that it's time to reseal, not a date on a calendar",
     "<p>Since there's no fixed number of years written into the sealer's own specification, the surface itself is the better clock to watch: water that used to bead up and roll off now soaks in, or the color that looked wet and rich has gone flat and faded. Either change is the practical signal that the old coat has worn through on a {city} driveway or patio, regardless of how many years have actually passed since the last sealing visit.</p>"),
    ("Keeping grit off a sealed surface between full cleanings",
     "<p>A light sweep and the occasional rinse between professional sealing visits keeps windblown sand and grit from scratching the sealer coat underfoot, since that fine abrasion is a slower but steadier wear factor than any single storm or cleaning. A {city} patio near an unpaved area or a driveway that collects blown sand from a neighboring lot benefits more from this habit than a surface set well back from loose ground, simply because there's more grit arriving on it in the first place.</p>"),
    ("Catching a single rocking paver before the next full service visit",
     "<p>A paver that starts to rock or sit slightly low between scheduled cleanings is worth flagging early, since resetting one unit is a small job compared with the repair needed once several pavers in the same area have shifted and the joints around them have opened up. A {city} homeowner who notices this between visits and calls it in before the next full re-sanding round usually ends up with a smaller bill and a surface that never develops a visible pattern of wear in that one corner.</p>"),
    ("Why some household cleaners do more harm than the weather does",
     "<p>Certain acidic or ammonia-based cleaners can dull or strip a sealer coat faster than ordinary sun and rain exposure would on their own, which matters for whatever gets used to rinse spills or mildew spots off a {city} surface between professional sealing visits. A mild, paver-safe cleaner and plain water handle most routine grime without working against the sealer itself. Checking a product's label against paver sealer compatibility before reaching for it is a small habit that protects how long the coat actually lasts.</p>"),
    ("Topping off a thin spot in the joints without waiting for the whole job",
     "<p>A handful of joints losing sand faster than the rest, often near a downspout, a heavily used path, or a shaded corner that stays damp longer, can usually be topped off by hand between full re-sanding visits rather than left until the entire surface needs attention again. Catching that kind of localized loss early on a {city} property keeps a small maintenance task from growing into a bigger one, and it protects the base underneath from the water that an open joint would otherwise let through.</p>"),
  ],
}
